HELP ( -- )
  HELP NAME PRINTS THIS FILE'S ENTRY FOR ONE WORD: TRY HELP HGR.
  THE ENTRIES LIVE IN HELPTEXT ON THE SYSTEM DISK, WHICH YOU CAN EDIT.
  CAT              LIST THE DISK
  N DRIVE          SWITCH DRIVES: 2 IS THE PROGRAMS DISK
  N LOCK           LOCK OR UNLOCK A FILE
  N DEL   N REN    DELETE OR RENAME ONE
  N LOAD           INTERPRET A TEXT FILE AS FORTH
  MARK  UNMARK     BRACKET WORK YOU MEAN TO THROW AWAY
  SAVEDICT         WRITE IT AS AN OVERLAY, N LOADDICT READS IT BACK
  ADDR LEN SAVE    WRITE A TEXT FILE, ASKING FOR THE NAME
  ADDR LEN BSAVE   THE SAME AS A BINARY FILE
  N ADDR BLOAD     READ ONE BACK, RETURNING ITS LENGTH
  WORDS            EVERY DEFINITION IN THE DICTIONARY
  HGR   TEXT       GRAPHICS SCREEN ON, AND BACK TO HERE
  N HCOLOR         0 BLACK 1 GREY 2 GREY 3 WHITE
  FLAG HXOR        DRAW BY XOR, SO DRAWING TWICE ERASES
  X Y HPLOT        X1 X2 Y HLINE        X1 Y1 X2 Y2 HLINE2
  X Y R HCIRCLE    X Y R HDISC          HCLS
  X Y HFILL        FLOOD THE REGION AROUND A POINT
  A W H X Y BLIT   DRAW A BITMAP, 8 PIXELS PER BYTE
  X1 X2 Y1 Y2 HBOX AND HFRAME
  COL ROW TAT      T. TEXT ON THE GRAPHICS SCREEN, FLAG TINV
  KEY? KEYC BTN    N PADDLE
  CLICK  P N TONE  N MS  VBL
  T S ADDR DREAD   T S ADDR DWRITE      RAW SECTORS
DRIVE ( N -- )
  SWITCH DRIVES AND RELOAD THE CATALOG.  1 IS THE SYSTEM DISK, 2 THE
  PROGRAMS DISK, AND 3 A RAM DISK ON A MEMORY CARD -- LIB RAMDISK.FTH
  FIRST.  CAT, LOAD AND THE FILE COMMANDS ALL MEAN THE CURRENT DRIVE.
CAT ( -- )
  LIST THE DISK: NUMBER, LOCK STAR, NAME, TYPE, SIZE IN SECTORS.
LOAD ( N -- )
  READ FILE N AS FORTH, AS IF YOU HAD TYPED IT.  THE NUMBER IS THE ONE
  CAT PRINTS, ON THE CURRENT DRIVE -- THE PROGRAMS ARE ON DRIVE 2, SO
  2 DRIVE FIRST, THEN CAT, THEN N LOAD.  DEFINITIONS MADE THIS WAY
  STAY UNTIL FORGET OR REBOOT.
SAVE ( ADDR LEN -- )
  WRITE LEN BYTES AT ADDR AS A TEXT FILE.  ASKS FOR THE NAME.
BSAVE ( ADDR LEN -- )
  THE SAME, AS A BINARY FILE WITH A LOAD ADDRESS HEADER.
BLOAD ( N ADDR -- LEN )
  READ BINARY FILE N TO ADDR, RETURNING ITS LENGTH.
DEL ( N -- )
  DELETE FILE N.  LOCKED FILES ARE REFUSED -- THAT IS WHAT THE LOCK IS
  FOR.  THE SECTORS GO BACK TO THE FREE BITMAP.
REN ( N -- )
  RENAME FILE N, ASKING FOR THE NEW NAME.
LOCK ( N -- )
  TOGGLE THE LOCK ON FILE N.  CAT SHOWS A STAR ON LOCKED FILES.
FREE ( -- U )
  HOW MANY SECTORS THE CURRENT DISK HAS FREE.
INIT ( -- )
  A FRESH EMPTY FILESYSTEM ON THE CURRENT DRIVE.  EVERY FILE IS LOST.
  ON DRIVE 1 THE SYSTEM'S OWN TRACKS ARE KEPT, SO IT STAYS BOOTABLE.
FINDF ( ADDR LEN -- N )
  THE CATALOG NUMBER OF THE FILE BEARING THAT NAME, OR -1.
  S" GFXLIB.FTH" FINDF LOAD
FOPEN ( N -- OK )
  OPEN FILE N FOR SEQUENTIAL READING.  ONE FILE AT A TIME.
FGETC ( -- C )
  THE NEXT BYTE OF THE OPEN FILE, OR -1 AT THE END.
FREAD ( ADDR N -- GOT )
  UP TO N BYTES OF THE OPEN FILE INTO ADDR.
DFGETS ( ADDR N -- GOT EOF )
  ONE LINE OF THE OPEN FILE INTO ADDR, AT MOST N CHARACTERS.
FCLOSE ( -- )
  DONE READING.
PICSAVE ( -- )
  WRITE THE GRAPHICS SCREEN AS TWO FILES, ONE PER BANK, ASKING FOR A
  NAME FOR EACH -- MAIN HALF FIRST, THEN AUXILIARY.  NEEDS 8K OF FREE
  DICTIONARY, TO STAGE THE AUXILIARY HALF.
PICLOAD ( NMAIN NAUX -- )
  READ A PICTURE BACK ONTO THE SCREEN, GIVING BOTH CATALOG NUMBERS.
SAVEDICT ( -- )
  WRITE EVERYTHING DEFINED SINCE MARK AS AN OVERLAY FILE.
  N LOADDICT READS IT BACK IN SECONDS, WITHOUT RECOMPILING.
MARK ( -- )
  REMEMBER WHERE THE DICTIONARY IS.  UNMARK THROWS AWAY EVERYTHING
  DEFINED SINCE.  SAVEDICT WRITES THAT SAME STRETCH TO DISK.
FORGET ( -- )
  FORGET NAME: THE NAMED WORD AND EVERYTHING DEFINED AFTER IT.
MARKER ( -- )
  MARKER NAME MAKES A WORD THAT, WHEN RUN, FORGETS ITSELF AND ALL
  THAT FOLLOWED IT.
WORDS ( -- )
  EVERY NAME IN THE DICTIONARY, IMMEDIATE ONES STARRED.
SEE ( -- )
  SEE NAME: DECOMPILE A COLON DEFINITION, ONE THREAD CELL PER LINE.
  SEE, DUMP AND MARKER RIDE ON DISK AND FETCH THEMSELVES WHEN NAMED.
DUMP ( ADDR U -- )
  MEMORY AS HEX AND CHARACTERS, SIXTEEN BYTES A ROW.
.S ( -- )
  PRINT THE WHOLE DATA STACK WITHOUT DISTURBING IT.
DEPTH ( -- U )
  HOW MANY CELLS ARE ON THE DATA STACK.
HGR ( -- )
  THE 560X192 GRAPHICS SCREEN, CLEARED.  DRAW WITH HPLOT, HLINE AND
  FRIENDS; TEXT COMES BACK TO THE CONSOLE.  SEE ALSO GR FOR LO-RES.
TEXT ( -- )
  BACK TO THE 80-COLUMN CONSOLE FROM EITHER GRAPHICS SCREEN.
HCOLOR ( N -- )
  DRAWING COLOUR: 0 BLACK, 1 AND 2 GREY, 3 WHITE.
HXOR ( FLAG -- )
  NONZERO: DRAWING FLIPS PIXELS INSTEAD OF SETTING THEM, SO DRAWING A
  SHAPE TWICE ERASES IT.  THAT IS HOW THINGS MOVE WITHOUT WRECKING THE
  BACKGROUND.  0 PUTS IT BACK.
HPLOT ( X Y -- )
  ONE PIXEL.  X RUNS 0-559, Y RUNS 0-191.
HPOINT ( X Y -- FLAG )
  IS THAT PIXEL SET?
HLINE ( X1 X2 Y -- )
  A HORIZONTAL SPAN.
HVLINE ( X Y1 Y2 -- )
  A VERTICAL ONE.
HLINE2 ( X1 Y1 X2 Y2 -- )
  A LINE OF ANY SLOPE.
HCIRCLE ( X Y R -- )
  A CIRCLE OUTLINE.  HDISC FILLS IT.
HDISC ( X Y R -- )
  A FILLED CIRCLE.
HFRAME ( X1 X2 Y1 Y2 -- )
  A RECTANGLE OUTLINE.  HBOX FILLS IT.
HBOX ( X1 X2 Y1 Y2 -- )
  A FILLED RECTANGLE.
HCLS ( -- )
  CLEAR THE GRAPHICS SCREEN.
HFILL ( X Y -- )
  FLOOD THE CONNECTED REGION AROUND THE POINT WITH THE CURRENT COLOUR.
BLIT ( ADDR W H X Y -- )
  DRAW A BITMAP, EIGHT PIXELS TO THE BYTE, W BYTES BY H ROWS.
BLK-SAVE ( ADDR X Y W H -- )
  COPY A BLOCK OF SCREEN INTO MEMORY.  BLK-REST PUTS IT BACK ANYWHERE.
TAT ( COL ROW -- )
  PLACE THE GRAPHICS TEXT CURSOR: 80 COLUMNS, 24 ROWS.
T. ( -- )
  T." LIKE THIS" PRINTS ON THE GRAPHICS SCREEN AT THE TAT POSITION.
TINV ( FLAG -- )
  NONZERO: GRAPHICS TEXT COMES OUT INVERSE.
GR ( -- )
  THE 40X48 SIXTEEN-COLOUR LO-RES SCREEN, WITH FOUR TEXT ROWS BELOW.
  GR-FULL TAKES THE WHOLE HEIGHT.  BLOCKY AND FAST -- CHARTS WANT
  THIS, NOT 560 PIXELS.
GCOLOR! ( N -- )
  LO-RES COLOUR, 0-15.
GPLOT ( X Y -- )
  ONE LO-RES BLOCK.  X RUNS 0-39, Y RUNS 0-47.
GSCRN ( X Y -- N )
  THE COLOUR ALREADY AT A BLOCK.
GHLIN ( X1 X2 Y -- )
  A LO-RES HORIZONTAL RUN.  GVLIN IS THE VERTICAL ONE.
GBAR ( X Y1 Y2 W -- )
  A FILLED LO-RES BAR, FOR CHARTS.
GCLS ( N -- )
  FILL THE WHOLE LO-RES SCREEN WITH COLOUR N.
S>F ( N -- )
  PUSH AN INTEGER ONTO THE FLOAT STACK.  OR JUST TYPE THE NUMBER WITH A
  POINT IN IT -- 3.14159 PUSHES A FLOAT -- WHICH WORKS AT THE PROMPT,
  THOUGH NOT YET INSIDE A COLON DEFINITION.  FOUR DECIMAL PLACES.
  THE FLOATS ARE APPLESOFT'S OWN FIVE-BYTE KIND, AND THE ARITHMETIC IS
  THE ROM'S.
F>S ( -- N )
  POP THE TOP FLOAT AS AN INTEGER.  IT FLOORS, THE WAY APPLESOFT'S INT
  DOES -- -2.5 COMES BACK -3, NOT -2, BECAUSE THE ROM DECIDES.  F>S+
  ADDS A HALF FIRST, WHICH ROUNDS.
F+ ( -- )
  ALSO F- F* F/ FSQRT FSIN FCOS FTAN FATAN FLN FEXP -- EACH WORKS ON
  THE FLOAT STACK.  FDUP FDROP FSWAP FOVER SHUFFLE IT; F. PRINTS.
F. ( -- )
  PRINT THE TOP FLOAT, APPLESOFT STYLE.
KEY ( -- C )
  WAIT FOR A KEYPRESS.
KEY? ( -- C )
  0 IF NOTHING IS PRESSED, THE CHARACTER IF SOMETHING IS.  FOR GAME
  LOOPS THAT MUST NOT STOP.
PADDLE ( N -- U )
  GAME PADDLE N, 0-255.
BTN ( -- FLAG )
  IS THE GAME BUTTON DOWN?
CLICK ( -- )
  ONE TICK OF THE SPEAKER.
TONE ( P N -- )
  N CYCLES AT PITCH P.  SMALLER P IS HIGHER.
MS ( N -- )
  WAIT ABOUT N MILLISECONDS.
VBL ( -- )
  WAIT FOR THE VERTICAL BLANK, FOR FLICKER-FREE ANIMATION.
DREAD ( T S ADDR -- ERR )
  ONE RAW SECTOR OFF THE DISK, NO FILESYSTEM.  0 MEANS IT WORKED.
DWRITE ( T S ADDR -- ERR )
  THE SAME, ONTO THE DISK.  NOTHING CHECKS WHAT YOU AIM AT.
CATCH ( XT -- 0 | N )
  RUN A WORD, CATCHING ANY THROW FROM INSIDE IT: 0 IF IT RETURNED,
  OTHERWISE THE THROWN VALUE, WITH BOTH STACKS PUT BACK.
THROW ( N -- )
  NONZERO: UNWIND TO THE NEAREST CATCH.
VARIABLE ( -- )
  VARIABLE NAME MAKES A CELL; NAME PUSHES ITS ADDRESS.  @ AND !
  READ AND WRITE IT.
CONSTANT ( N -- )
  N CONSTANT NAME; NAME THEREAFTER PUSHES N.
CREATE ( -- )
  CREATE NAME MAKES A WORD PUSHING ITS OWN DATA ADDRESS; ALLOT AND ,
  PUT DATA THERE.  DOES> GIVES THE DATA BEHAVIOUR.
ALLOT ( N -- )
  RESERVE N BYTES OF DICTIONARY AT HERE.
HERE ( -- ADDR )
  THE NEXT FREE DICTIONARY BYTE.
UNUSED ( -- U )
  HOW MUCH DICTIONARY IS LEFT.
PARSE-NAME ( -- ADDR LEN )
  THE NEXT WORD ON THE INPUT LINE, LENGTH 0 IF THERE IS NONE.  HOW
  HELP READS ITS ARGUMENT.
EMIT ( C -- )
  PRINT ONE CHARACTER.
TYPE ( ADDR LEN -- )
  PRINT A STRING.
CR ( -- )
  END THE LINE.
PAGE ( -- )
  CLEAR THE TEXT SCREEN.
. ( N -- )
  PRINT A NUMBER AND A SPACE.  U. IS UNSIGNED, .R RIGHT-JUSTIFIES.
BASE ( -- ADDR )
  THE NUMBER BASE, 10 AT BOOT.  16 BASE ! FOR HEX; A LEADING $ READS
  HEX WHATEVER BASE SAYS.
EDIT ( -- )
  A LINE EDITOR, SO YOU CAN WRITE CODE ON THE MACHINE.  ON THE PROGRAMS
  DISK: 2 DRIVE, THEN LOAD IT, THEN EHELP.
  NEW  +L  LIST  N INS  N SET  N DEL-L  N EREAD  EWRITE
GFXLIB ( -- )
  ARCS, ELLIPSES, PIE SLICES AND BLOCK SAVE/RESTORE.  LIB GFXLIB.FTH
  LOADS IT FROM EITHER DRIVE.  HARC HPIE HELLIPSE HPOLY BLK-SAVE
  BLK-REST F>S+
POINTER ( -- )
  AN EVENT QUEUE AND HIT TESTING FOR THE GAME PORT, ON THE PROGRAMS
  DISK.  A PRESS BECOMES AN EV-DOWN AT COORDINATES, AND HOT-FIND SAYS
  WHICH REGION IT LANDED IN.
DRV ( -- ADDR )
  WHICH DRIVE IS CURRENT, 1 2 OR 3.  DRIVE SETS IT; THE FILE WORDS
  READ IT.
LIB ( -- )
  LIB GFXLIB.FTH LOADS A LIBRARY OFF THE SYSTEM DISK BY NAME, FROM
  EITHER DRIVE, AND PUTS YOUR DRIVE BACK.  LIKE LOAD, IT IS FOR THE
  PROMPT, NOT FOR THE MIDDLE OF A FILE BEING LOADED.
RAMDISK ( -- )
  TYPE RAMDISK AND A RAMWORKS MEMORY CARD BECOMES DRIVE 3: A FULL
  560-SECTOR DISK AT MEMORY SPEED, FORMATTED ON FIRST USE, FORGOTTEN
  AT POWER-OFF.  IT REPORTS THE CARD -- UP TO 128 BANKS, 8 MB.
  FAST SCRATCH, NOT SAFE KEEPING.
STR ( -- )
  LOADS THE STRING WORDS.  A STRING IS AN ADDRESS AND A LENGTH, WHICH
  IS WHAT S" LEAVES AND WHAT TYPE TAKES.
  A1 L1 A2 L2 S=        IS THE TEXT THE SAME
  A L START N SUB       MID$      A L N LEFT      A L N RIGHT
  A L C SCAN            WHERE A CHARACTER IS, -1 IF NOWHERE
  A L C SPLIT           HEAD AND TAIL EITHER SIDE OF IT
  A L TRIM              BLANKS OFF BOTH ENDS       A L UPPER
  A L S>N               VAL, WITH A FLAG SAYING IF IT WAS A NUMBER
  N N>S                 STR$
  N SBUF                ONE OF EIGHT 64-BYTE SLOTS TO KEEP ONE IN
  A L DEST SCOPY        A1 L1 A2 L2 DEST SCAT
MORE ( N -- )
  PAGE THROUGH TEXT FILE N, TWENTY-TWO LINES AT A TIME.  ANY KEY
  TURNS THE PAGE, Q STOPS.  JUST TYPE IT -- THE SYSTEM FETCHES THE
  TOOL FROM THE SYSTEM DISK THE FIRST TIME IT IS NAMED.
PICK ( -- )
  CHOOSE A PROGRAM OFF THIS DRIVE WITH A HIGHLIGHTED BAR: ARROWS MOVE
  IT, RETURN LOADS IT, ESCAPE BACKS OUT.  TYPE MENUS AND IT LOADS
  ITSELF.  ONLY THE FIRST TWENTY FILES ARE SHOWN.
MENU-PICK ( N TOPROW -- CHOICE )
  RUN THE BAR OVER N ITEMS STARTING AT ROW TOPROW, RETURNING WHICH WAS
  CHOSEN OR -1 FOR ESCAPE.  'MI HOLDS A WORD ( I -- ADDR LEN ) THAT
  GIVES THE TEXT OF ITEM I.
CTYPE ( ADDR LEN COL ROW -- )
  WRITE A STRING ON THE CONSOLE AT A PLACE YOU CHOOSE.  FLAG CINV FIRST
  FOR INVERSE.  UNDERNEATH ARE THE KERNEL'S CPUT AND CINV.
CPUT ( C COL ROW -- )
  ONE CHARACTER ON THE CONSOLE, BYPASSING THE FIRMWARE -- WHICH IS THE
  ONLY WAY TO GET INVERSE, SINCE COUT SETS THE HIGH BIT AND THE HIGH BIT
  IS WHAT MAKES TEXT NORMAL.  FLAG CINV CHOOSES.
MENU ( -- )
  LIST THE PROGRAMS DISK AND ASK WHICH NUMBER TO LOAD.  JUST TYPE
  IT -- THE TOOL FETCHES ITSELF THE FIRST TIME IT IS NAMED.
LIFE ( -- )
  CONWAY'S GAME OF LIFE ON THE LO-RES SCREEN: LIFE RUNS A RANDOM
  SOUP UNTIL ANY KEY, GLIDER LIFE SENDS THE FIVE-CELL SHIP, GEN
  STEPS ONE GENERATION BY HAND.
PAINT ( -- )
  FORTHPAINT: THE PADDLES AIM A CROSSHAIR, THE BUTTON INKS.  KEYS
  1-5 PICK PEN, LINE, BOX, DISC, FILL; W AND E PICK INK; X IS XOR;
  S SAVES THE PICTURE AS TWO FILES AND n1 n2 RESUME BRINGS IT BACK.
  JUST TYPE PAINT -- IT FETCHES ITSELF.
WRITE ( -- )
  FORTHWRITE: COMPOSE PROSE WITH WORD WRAP.  RETURN ENDS A
  PARAGRAPH, DELETE RUBS OUT, ESC FINISHES.  WLIST READS IT BACK,
  WSAVE KEEPS IT, n WLOAD CONTINUES A SAVED ONE.
LEDGER ( -- )
  A CHECKBOOK IN EXACT CENTS: 1250 00 IN SALARY TAKES IN $1250.00,
  3 15 OUT COFFEE PAYS $3.15, BAL. AND ENTRIES SHOW THE MONEY,
  LSAVE KEEPS THE BOOK AS A TEXT FILE.
LANDER ( -- )
  LUNAR LANDER, ON THE PROGRAMS DISK: 2 DRIVE, CAT, N LOAD, LANDER.
  A OR THE BUTTON BURNS, Z AND X STEER.  LAND ON THE PAD, SLOWLY.
BREAKOUT ( -- )
  ON THE PROGRAMS DISK: 2 DRIVE, CAT, N LOAD, BREAKOUT.  THE PADDLE
  KNOB IS THE BAT.  THE GAME WOZ BUILT THE HARDWARE FOR.
HAT ( -- )
  ON THE PROGRAMS DISK: 2 DRIVE, CAT, N LOAD, HAT.  THE CLASSIC SINE
  SURFACE, DRAWN BY THE ROM'S OWN FLOATING POINT.  TWO MINUTES.
DATA: ( -- )
  DATA: NAME 1 , 2 , 3 , ;DATA MAKES AN INLINE TABLE; N READ-VAL
  FETCHES, RESTORE-DATA REWINDS.  THE BASIC READER'S DATA, ROUGHLY.
